On Sun, Nov 6, 2011 at 9:11 AM, Kevin Reid <kpreid@switchb.org> wrote:
>
> For language acquisition, shouldn't we prefer sentences with "simple words" over ones with lujvo, when the lujvo are there for a "variant" meaning rather than one that's fundamentally not a gismu?

If you want the acquirer to acquire the full morphology, you need to
use a good sample of lujvo, fu'ivla and cmevla too. I understand
infants acquire the phonological rules of a language before they
acquire any significant vocabulary, so hearing full blown "grown up"
speech is important for them too.

